  "headline": "Grim FC seizes the summit as Louis Grimoldby posts a league-best 98",
  "summary": "Louis Grimoldby stormed into first with a gameweek-leading 98, taking Grim FC to 311 points and a 5-point lead over Tim Chodha’s Hakuna Juan Mata. Tim’s 96 kept the pressure firmly on, while former leader Will Finney slipped to third after a 79-point wildcard.",
  "report": "There is a new leader of the Biddenham Fantasy League. Louis Grimoldby’s Grim FC delivered the gameweek’s top score of 98, moving up one place to 311 overall and taking the title-race lead. Tim Chodha was right alongside him on 96 for Hakuna Juan Mata, but remains 5 points back on 306; Will Finney’s 79-point wildcard leaves 0v3rf1tt3d.h5 third on 298 after a two-place fall. With 34 gameweeks remaining, the top is already serving up proper drama.\n\nLouis was manager of the week with a clean 98 and no hits, comfortably ahead of the 76.1 league average and the 67 global average. His captaincy call on João Pedro, worth 12 points with a multiplier of 2, was the decisive touch, while the one-owned Dunk chipped in 12 points. Louis is also the league’s hottest manager over the last three gameweeks, with 258 net points, so this move to the summit has not come from nowhere.\n\nTim’s 96 ensured the new leader got no chance to relax, while olly barnes completed the gameweek podium on 88. Grasshoppers surged four places into fourth on 291, just 2 points above Mark Horton’s Vespa Mo Pedro on 289; Mark, in turn, is only 2 ahead of Mike Flynn in sixth. It is close enough around the top five that every captaincy call is already looking rather important.\n\nThe captaincy template was Haaland, chosen by 12 managers for 9 points. Louis went one better with João Pedro, but olly barnes had to make do with Isak’s 2-point howler despite the excellent 88-point wildcard. The other wildcards belonged to Will Finney, who scored 79, and David Bailey, who scored 80. James Geary’s freehit returned 75, while Cathy Eckett-Brown’s freehit brought 55 and an eight-place drop.\n\nAway from the headline acts, amy warden was the league’s biggest climber, gaining 8 places after an 88-point week powered by the one-owned Bogle’s 15 points. George Foy rose 7 places with 83, helped by Mykolenko’s one-owned 11, while Callum Bowness climbed 6 after Belloumi’s one-owned 13 contributed to an 82. Phil Johnson also found a rare return in Gvardiol’s 11, and Tavernier’s 8 was shared only by James Marshall and olly barnes.\n\nDavid Bailey’s 80-point wildcard had a painful 20 points sitting on the bench, a bench agony total that will linger. At the other end, David Tonks took the wooden spoon: Net six and chill scored 57 before a 4-point hit reduced it to 53, producing a seven-place fall. Ali Smith also took a 4-point hit, turning 78 gross into 74 net. Paul Ray’s 58 meant a five-place fall, while Paul Alexis remains the coldest manager across the last three gameweeks on 179 net points.\n\nFor perspective, even Louis’s excellent 98 remains short of Mark Horton’s season record of 116 from gameweek 2, while the global high this week was 151. Still, with Grim FC ahead by 5, fourth and fifth split by 2, and fifth and sixth split by another 2, the BFL table is not offering anyone much breathing room.",
